INFO:__main__:Prompt Metadata:
INFO:__main__:- name: AIAH Get Action Prompt
INFO:__main__:- description: This prompt is for the AIAH to use its knowledge (memory, consience and skills) and the environment status to take an action. This action will impact with environment
INFO:__main__:- requires: AIAH_memory, AIAH_skills, AIAH_conscience, ES_enviroment_status
INFO:httpx:HTTP Request: POST https://api.x.ai/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
INFO:__main__:{    "action": "I find myself standing amidst rolling hills that slowly give way to a denser cluster of trees. The air feels cool against my skin, and a soft breeze brushes past me as the light begins to fade with the approaching evening. A small stream cuts through the land nearby, its gentle sound reaching my ears. My body feels a bit weary, and a gnawing sensation in my stomach tells me I need to eat soon. My mouth is dry, though not painfully so, and I feel a slight ache in my limbs, perhaps from the day's exertions or minor scrapes I can't quite recall. Despite this, I feel alert enough to take in my surroundings.    Looking around, I notice the stream with its clear water. My first instinct is to approach it and quench my thirst, as the dryness in my throat is bothersome. I walk carefully down to the water's edge, feeling the uneven ground beneath my feet, and kneel to scoop some water into my hands. The coolness of it against my lips feels refreshing, and I drink until the dryness fades. I reason that water is essential, and since it looks clean and is easily accessible, this is a safe choice to address one of my immediate needs.    Next, I turn my attention to the growing hunger in my stomach. I see plants in the woodland that look plentiful, though I’m unsure which are safe to eat since I have no memory or knowledge to guide me. I also notice small rodents scurrying about and hear the splash of fish in the stream, though they seem wary of me and harder to catch near where I stand. Given my current energy level, which feels somewhat low, I decide against chasing after animals for now. Instead, I choose to cautiously explore the nearby woodland for plants. I pick a few leaves and berries that look appealing, but I only take small nibbles at first, waiting to see if my body reacts badly to them. I reason that if they cause no immediate discomfort, they might be safe to eat in small amounts to ease my hunger.    I’m currently under a natural overhang in the hills, which provides some shelter. With evening approaching and the temperature already cool, I feel a slight chill on my skin. I decide to gather some branches or leaves from the nearby trees to create a barrier against the breeze and perhaps insulate my shelter a bit more. I do this slowly, as my energy isn’t high, and I don’t want to overexert myself. My reasoning is that staying warm and protected during the night is important, especially since I feel minor aches and don’t want my condition to worsen.    As I work, I remain aware of the wildlife around me. The small herbivores and birds don’t seem threatening, and the foxes I glimpse in the distance appear cautious rather than aggressive. I decide to keep my movements slow and non-threatening, reasoning that avoiding startling them is the best way to stay safe given the very low danger level of my surroundings.    Reflecting on my choices, I believe I’ve prioritized my immediate needs—water, food, and shelter—based on what my body is telling me and what I can observe around me. I’m unsure about the plants I’ve eaten, and I’ll need to pay attention to how my body feels afterward. I also recognize that my energy is limited, so I’ve avoided tasks that seem too demanding, like catching fish or rodents. I hope that by conserving my strength and securing basic necessities, I’ll be in a better position to face whatever comes next.    From this experience, I’ve learned:    1. Water from a clear stream can relieve thirst and feels refreshing, so it’s a priority when my throat is dry.    2. Hunger can be addressed by cautiously trying small amounts of plants, though I must watch for negative reactions since I don’t know what’s safe.    3. Shelter can be improved with natural materials like branches and leaves to protect against cool temperatures and wind.    4. Wildlife that appears non-aggressive can be observed without immediate fear, but I should move carefully to avoid startling them.    5. My body’s signals—like hunger, thirst, and low energy—guide my decisions, and I need to listen to them to avoid overexertion."}
