o recall from morning foraging lessons that timing is critical, and while early afternoon light still offers safety, my low energy suggests waiting for a better moment to explore deeper woodland. The frustration of not being able to hunt rodents or fish due to their wariness and my limitations, as noted in late morning reflections, further convinces me to avoid wasting energy on unlikely successes now.\n\nEmotionally, I feel a mix of mild frustration at my physical limitations and the scarcity of immediate food, but also relief in knowing I have a secure shelter and accessible water to fall back on. There\u2019s a lingering concern about hunger, but I trust that resting now will position me better for future foraging when energy is higher. I\u2019m also slightly anxious about nocturnal predators showing mild curiosity, but since they\u2019re inactive during daylight, I feel safe enough to rest at the overhang.\n\nLessons learned from this decision include the importance of pacing myself and recognizing when low energy necessitates rest over action, even in relatively safe conditions. I\u2019ve also reinforced the value of using downtime for passive observation of wildlife, which may build knowledge for future hunting or fishing attempts. Lastly, I\u2019ve noted that resource scarcity in immediate areas means I\u2019ll need to plan for broader exploration soon, but only when my energy allows it.",
